data.content:
# EF Core 10 Breaking Changes for Your Code

## 1. `SetProperty` — value parameter must now be a lambda expression

In EF Core 10, `SetProperty` no longer has an overload accepting a raw `TProperty` value. Both arguments must be `Expression<Func<TSource, TProperty>>` lambdas. Your `Expression.Constant("foo")` won't resolve.

**Fix:** Wrap the constant in a lambda expression:

```csharp
var blogParameter = Expression.Parameter(typeof(Blog), "b");
var valueParameter = Expression.Parameter(typeof(Blog), "b2");

setters = Expression.Lambda<Func<SetPropertyCalls<Blog>, SetPropertyCalls<Blog>>>(
    Expression.Call(
        instance: setters.Body,
        methodName: nameof(SetPropertyCalls<Blog>.SetProperty),
        typeArguments: new[] { typeof(string) },
        arguments: new Expression[]
        {
            Expression.Lambda<Func<Blog, string>>(
                Expression.Property(blogParameter, nameof(Blog.Name)), blogParameter),
            // Wrap the value in a lambda instead of Expression.Constant
            Expression.Lambda<Func<Blog, string>>(
                Expression.Constant("foo"), valueParameter)
        }),
    setters.Parameters);
```

---

## 2. Complex types — duplicate column names now error by default

EF Core 10 uses the complex property name as a column prefix by default. If your existing schema has flat column names, the model won't match.

**Fix:** Explicitly configure column names to preserve the existing schema:

```csharp
modelBuilder.Entity<Customer>(b =>
{
    b.ComplexProperty(c => c.ShippingAddress, ab =>
    {
        ab.Property(a => a.Street).HasColumnName("ShippingStreet");
    });
    b.ComplexProperty(c => c.BillingAddress, ab =>
    {
        ab.Property(a => a.Street).HasColumnName("BillingStreet");
    });
});

// For nested: explicitly set each level
modelBuilder.Entity<Order>(b =>
{
    b.ComplexProperty(o => o.Payment, pb =>
    {
        pb.ComplexProperty(p => p.BillingAddress, ab =>
        {
            ab.Property(a => a.City).HasColumnName("Payment_BillingAddress_City");
        });
    });
});
```

Or, to restore the old convention globally, configure `ModelBuilder` with:

```csharp
modelBuilder.Model.SetPropertyDiscoveryConvention(/* old convention */);
```

---

## 3. `IRelationalCommandDiagnosticsLogger` — new `CommandSource` parameter

EF Core 10 added a `CommandSource` parameter to the diagnostics interface methods.

**Fix:** Add the new parameter:

```csharp
public class MyLogger : IRelationalCommandDiagnosticsLogger
{
    public InterceptionResult<DbDataReader> CommandReaderExecuting(
        IRelationalConnection connection,
        DbCommand command,
        DbContext context,
        Guid commandId,
        Guid connectionId,
        DateTimeOffset startTime,
        CommandSource commandSource)   // <-- new in EF Core 10
    { /* logging */ }
}
```

---

## Summary

| Issue | Root cause | Fix |
|-------|-----------|-----|
| `SetProperty` won't compile | Value overload removed; both args must be lambdas | Wrap value in `Expression.Lambda` |
| Complex type column mismatch | Default naming now prefixes with property name | Use `HasColumnName()` explicitly |
| Diagnostics interface error | `CommandSource` param added | Add parameter to implementation |
